# Rule: every toast is a title over one line, timed or persistent

The app raised 161 toasts in nine shapes before the redesign: one-line toasts and two-line ones, an
action as an inline link on some and a button on others, buttons on toasts that closed in four
seconds while the user reached for them, and "Failed to", "Could not" and "Couldn't" for the same
kind of failure. A card that took clicks with nothing to do on them once swallowed the Board
Manager's Save. `addToast` (`stores/toast-store.ts`) now takes one shape, and the type holds it.

## The rule

- **Every toast has a `title` and a body (`message`).** The title says what happened in a few
  words (a translated title that runs past the 380px card wraps to a second line instead of
  being cut off); the body gives the task, the place or the detail, as one sentence that fits one line at
  the card's 380px width. Only a task or file name may make it wrap. A reason main or the error
  wrote may follow as a second sentence (`asSentence`). A failure's title says what did not
  happen, most often "Couldn't ..." ("Couldn't resume session", "Automation failed"), and its body
  is the reason.
- **A timed toast carries no button.** It closes on its own (`durationMs`, the Notifications
  setting by default), and a button on it can leave while the user reaches for it. When the toast
  is about one thing, give it `onOpen`: the card opens that thing (a task through
  `openTaskFromNotification`, a list, a workflow, a folder) on a click.
- **A toast with a button is `persistent: true`.** Its `action` carries a label and an icon
  (`review`, `settings`, `retry`, `update`), drawn at the card's bottom right. A persistent toast
  may also have no button (its card opens its subject, or it closes when what it reports ends).
- **Change a toast in place with `updateToast`**, never dismiss-and-add for a count that grows.
- **Do not reintroduce a clickable card that opens nothing.** A card without `onOpen` is
  `pointer-events: none` and only its buttons take clicks. While a dialog, the settings panel or the
  search palette is open (`useRegisterOpenOverlay`, `utils/overlay-gate.ts`), nothing on a toast
  takes a click. A new modal overlay registers there.
- **The countdown is the store's, not a keyframe's.** A timed toast's remaining time lives in the
  toast store (held while the pointer is over it, kept across a Fast Refresh); the bottom line only
  draws it. `.no-motion` zeroes CSS animation durations, so no CSS animation may be the clock.

## Enforcement (self-maintaining)

- **Type system:** `ToastInput` is a union of a timed input (no `action`) and a persistent one, and
  `title` is required on both, so `npm run typecheck` fails a toast without a title or a button on
  a timed toast.
- **Tests:** `tests/unit/toast-store-hmr.test.ts` pins the store (the countdown hold and release,
  `updateToast`, the stack limit that never evicts a persistent toast for a timed one);
  `tests/ui/toast-behavior.spec.ts` pins the card (title over body, the countdown line, the hover
  hold on a click-through card, a card click that opens, the dialog gate including a panel opened by
  keyboard, the stepped line with Animations off, a toast in a pop-out);
  `tests/ui/toast-click-through.spec.ts` pins that a card that opens nothing never eats a click on
  what is under it.
- **Review:** the copy (one sentence, one line, "Couldn't") is judgment; `/code-review` checks it
  against [[writing-style]].

## Scope

Toasts raised in the renderer, the main window's and every pop-out's. Main never raises a toast:
it pushes the facts, and the renderer titles them (a body main composes keeps to one sentence).
